"""Original national office scenes; authored alternatives, never official history.

No named official person, historical disappearance, territorial outcome or
future conflict is introduced. Records read the player's actual policy/reply
flags. A lost/expired message never blocks the domestic service chain.
"""
from reconstruction.content import BRANCHES, COUNTRY_NAMES, branch_id, focus_id

OPENINGS = {
    "NPT": ("An Empty Chair at the Harbour Hearing",
        "The clerk has left one chair empty. It was reserved for an office that has promised staff to three different committees, and nobody can say which promise takes precedence. A harbour superintendent brings the receiving book instead of a speech. Beside each reservation she has written the name of the service that must actually provide it. Several lines have no name at all.\n\nA representative asks whether publishing the gaps would weaken the islands' bargaining position. The superintendent answers that a promise already becomes weaker when a ship arrives and the receiving crew is elsewhere. Another official warns that a public hearing can consume the very staff it hopes to coordinate. Both objections survive the meeting.\n\nThe government can make a public opening statement or begin with a narrower ministerial docket. Neither approach settles access obligations, harbour allocations or air-watch duties. It decides how the first questions will be asked, while the separate national charters will decide what the services can promise.",
        ("Open the hearing to public questions.", "Begin with a ministerial working docket.")),
    "REC": ("The Register That Came Back",
        "A district messenger returns a repair register with a strip of paper folded between its pages. The strip names a household whose request appears as completed in one return and unanswered in another. The messenger has not brought a territorial petition or a demand for a new government. She wants an office that will take responsibility for correcting the contradiction.\n\nAt the table, a council secretary argues that public challenges are the only way to discover such errors. The works superintendent worries that an unrestricted hearing will turn every delayed job into a national dispute before crews have time to inspect it. The ledger contains evidence for both concerns: a helpful local correction on one page, a requisition with no receiving signature on the next.\n\nThe republic can open the first hearing or ask its offices to reconcile a limited docket before reporting. The choice will be remembered when the new records, works and external-services branches are assessed. The inherited settlement remains the boundary within which this ordinary work must succeed.",
        ("Hear the first claims in public.", "Give the offices a bounded reconciliation docket.")),
    "SAP": ("The Appropriation Left on the Table",
        "The committee's final folder is thinner than the speeches that introduced it. It contains a rota, a list of outstanding training returns and a request to reserve technical staff for another period. A member of the chamber asks how many public commitments depend on those same names. The secretary turns the sheet over and begins a second list.\n\nAn air-service inspector objects to the implication that unreported work is necessarily wasted work. Some records protect the people who submit them, and some lessons need a smaller room before they can be discussed honestly. The member agrees, but points to an appropriation that has been renewed without a receiving report. Discretion has begun to resemble an absence of questions.\n\nThe government can invite a public opening statement or require a ministerial docket first. Subsequent national choices will decide the balance between civilian review, protected readiness and professional debrief. Today's procedure supplies a voice for that argument; it does not settle it by acclaim.",
        ("Ask the chamber to state the questions publicly.", "Commission a ministerial account before the hearing.")),
    "GEB": ("A Request without a Receiving Office",
        "The request is polite, carefully stamped and addressed to an office that no longer keeps the relevant duty book. A frontier official walks it to the recovery administration, which returns it with a second address. By the afternoon it has acquired three signatures and still has nobody authorized to answer.\n\nA district representative calls the delay a failure of independence: a country should be able to give a useful refusal as readily as a useful acceptance. The dispatch officer answers that an immediate reply can promise crews already assigned to essential repairs. The argument is interrupted when a junior clerk produces an older letter containing precisely that promise. Its receiving report has never arrived.\n\nThe cabinet can open its first review to public questions or begin with a protected ministerial docket. Neither creates access through another country or reopens a frontier. The receiving-authority and recovery branches will determine who can commit the nation's staff, and who must explain when the answer is no.",
        ("Put the unanswered requests before a public review.", "Start with a protected ministerial docket.")),
}

SCENES = {
    "NPT_mandate": ("The Reservation Written in Pencil",
        "A visitor's request reaches the committee with the word routine underlined twice. The facilities officer has attached a list of existing obligations and a pencil note: routine for whom? There is space in the calendar, but accepting the request would consume the inspection team scheduled to verify another commitment.\n\nThe chair refuses to treat the new charter as a permission slip or a permanent veto. One member wants a published return so that the next hearing can see exactly what was reserved. Another asks for a protected file, arguing that officials will disclose practical limits more honestly before their tentative estimates become public promises. The facilities officer supports neither position without qualification. She wants the final instruction to name who can change the reservation, and who must receive notice when it changes.\n\nThe chosen national mandate remains in force. This reply decides how its first working record will be kept.",
        ("Publish the reservation and its limits.", "Protect the working estimates in a ministerial file."),
        "A Charter with a Renewal Date",
        "The same request returns as an example in the final review. Its pencil reservation has been replaced by a receiving instruction, an inspection date and a person responsible for informing the committee if the timetable fails. Nobody claims that the paperwork creates spare capacity. Its achievement is more modest: a promise can now be changed before somebody relies on it.\n\nThe facilities officer keeps the original pencil note in the file. A later government may choose different terms, she says, but it should inherit the question rather than discover it again at the berth. The settlement therefore preserves the national policy and its continuing cost while making renewal an ordinary procedure."),
    "NPT_harbours": ("Two Ships on One Receiving Sheet",
        "The harbour superintendent places two arrival sheets side by side. Each assumes that the same receiving crew will be free at the same hour. Neither captain has acted improperly; the contradiction began ashore, when separate offices counted a reservation as available capacity.\n\nThe new berthing policy tells the superintendent which window has priority. It does not tell her how much of the disagreement should enter the public account. A commercial representative argues that the next season's schedules depend on knowing what was withheld. A service officer fears that every tentative maintenance estimate will become a commitment the staff cannot revise. They agree on one point: deleting either sheet would produce a cleaner account and a worse harbour.\n\nThe inspection return can be published with its limitations or retained as a working file for the responsible offices. Either route must leave the conflicting demands visible to the people who allocate the next rota.",
        ("Publish the competing reservations.", "Keep a protected joint maintenance file."),
        "The Berth That Was Released on Time",
        "The useful line in the completed return is a cancellation. An office released its reservation before another service had to abandon a receiving window, and the harbour staff recorded who authorized the change. The superintendent calls it the least impressive success the new arrangement could have produced. It is also the one she wants repeated.\n\nThe final compact preserves the chosen civilian or defence window rather than declaring every demand equally urgent. Repair crews still have finite hours, and accepting exceptional traffic still displaces ordinary work. The settlement gives those costs a receiving office and a public responsibility, so the next promise can be assessed before it is made."),
    "NPT_airspace": ("A Quiet Channel Is Still a Channel",
        "The watch officer waits until the shift ends before taking her notes to the review. Nothing dramatic has occurred. A routine report reached the correct office, an airfield query received an answer, and the civil contact was warned before its channel was reserved for other work. She worries that the quiet return will look like evidence that the service was unnecessary.\n\nA communications representative asks for the record to be published in plain language. The air-service delegate prefers an internal debrief where operators can admit uncertainty without seeing an unfinished estimate repeated as a warning. The watch officer objects when either side describes silence as consent. A contact who does not answer must remain an unanswered contact in both records.\n\nThe government has already chosen its warning or joint-service policy. The reply now determines whether this first period is explained publicly or examined within a protected professional return.",
        ("Explain the quiet watch in a public return.", "Hold a protected operator debrief."),
        "The Duty Book at the Next Shift",
        "The final review begins with a change of shift. The incoming officer can find the receiving contact, the reservation that remains active and the question the previous team could not settle. The duty book does not pretend that uncertainty disappeared during the service period. It shows where responsibility continued despite it.\n\nThe settlement retains the selected warning or cooperation policy with its cost in staff attention and operational flexibility. Its practical conclusion is a service that can be handed over without losing the civilian contact or assuming that a foreign request has been accepted. The watch officer asks that the unanswered line remain in the book until somebody can answer it."),
    "REC_records": ("The Receipt without a Signature",
        "A district official brings a requisition receipt to the new review table. The quantity is clear, the destination is legible and the receiving signature is missing. A council delegate recognizes the allocation but says its office received only part of it. The official can account for the dispatch and cannot account for the difference.\n\nUnder the selected register policy, the administration must preserve the disagreement rather than resolve it by choosing the more convenient return. One inspector wants the disputed page opened to public challenge. Another argues that staff will stop recording uncertainty if every provisional note is treated as an accusation. The delegate answers that residents already live with the uncertainty, whether or not the page is published.\n\nThe first casework record can enter a public return or a protected inspection file. Neither answer changes the inherited settlement or grants a district what the receiving evidence cannot support.",
        ("Publish the disputed page for challenge.", "Protect the inspection file while evidence is gathered."),
        "The Page That Was Not Rewritten",
        "The final register contains both the original requisition and the correction. A clerk proposes replacing them with a single clean entry, but the district office asks that the history remain. The missing signature explains why a procedure changed; without it, the next administrator may mistake the change for unnecessary caution.\n\nThe settlement preserves either public review or accountable central control. It does not erase the burden each places on the administration. Its success is a record that can survive disagreement and still identify the office responsible for the next action. The household whose request began the review appears on the receiving sheet, rather than merely in an assurance that the matter was handled."),
    "REC_works": ("A Crew Asked to Be in Two Districts",
        "The works superintendent draws a line through a proposed dispatch time, then leaves it visible. Moving the crew would satisfy the most urgent request on the national sheet and interrupt maintenance another district has already planned around. The new allocation policy establishes a priority. It cannot make the second obligation disappear.\n\nA municipal representative wants the competing work orders published so that residents can see why one repair waits. The central dispatcher prefers a protected working return, warning that a tentative estimate can harden into a political deadline before an inspection is complete. A technician asks both offices to include the travelling and receiving time, which neither original estimate allowed.\n\nThe reply determines how the first maintenance return is examined. It must preserve the uncompleted work as a real obligation, whether the selected works policy protects a district allocation or concentrates crews under a central schedule.",
        ("Publish the competing work orders.", "Keep a protected technical dispatch return."),
        "The Crew's Ordinary Return",
        "The final report follows the crew back to ordinary maintenance. That return matters to the superintendent more than the ceremonial completion of the priority job: a service that never releases its staff cannot keep the promises made in the next district's calendar. The receiving office signs for the handover and notes the work still outstanding.\n\nThe settlement keeps the chosen municipal or central policy, including its continuing burden on political attention or civilian resources. Reconstruction becomes a normal service obligation with a record of what crews delivered and what they must still do. The crossed-out dispatch time remains attached as a reminder that a realistic promise is sometimes a slower one."),
    "REC_neighbours": ("A Useful Answer Can Be No",
        "The external-services desk receives a carefully worded request for compatible procedures. Its first draft reply thanks the other office and promises a timetable. The standards inspector returns the draft with one question: has anybody confirmed that the receiving office still has the authority and staff named in the request?\n\nA liaison official argues for publishing the unanswered conditions. Doing so would make a refusal understandable to the people whose services might otherwise be reserved. Another asks for a protected file that lets the offices correct assumptions without turning a practical delay into a diplomatic test. The inspector insists that domestic work continue while the answer is uncertain.\n\nThe national policy remains either compatible civil standards or protected national control. The first liaison return can be public or ministerial, but it cannot record a bilateral commitment that no eligible partner has accepted.",
        ("Publish the conditions for a useful answer.", "Keep a protected standards correspondence file."),
        "A Desk That Can Continue without an Offer",
        "At the final review the inspector presents a domestic receiving return alongside the external correspondence. The former is complete even where the latter remains unanswered. A liaison official initially calls that a disappointing result. The district representative answers that residents received a service which did not have to wait for another government's timetable.\n\nThe settlement preserves the chosen policy toward external procedures and a national alternative. It claims neither a treaty nor a change in alignment. Its contribution is an office that can give a useful answer, explain a refusal and keep ordinary technical work moving while the correspondence remains open."),
    "SAP_mandate": ("The Cost beneath the Signature",
        "The appropriations clerk places the signed undertaking above its staff return. A member of the chamber reads the first page aloud, then asks why the second was not attached when the undertaking was renewed. The cabinet secretary answers that the service was considered routine. The clerk has marked three routine assignments against the same receiving team.\n\nThe selected mandate determines which claim receives protection. A committee member wants the burden published so that civilian priorities can be questioned with the same seriousness as readiness. A defence representative asks for a protected working return in which a provisional estimate can be corrected before it becomes a public deadline. Neither disputes that the signature commits somebody else's hours.\n\nThe first service review can be public or ministerial. It will remain part of the record when the government decides which commitments deserve renewal.",
        ("Attach the staff return to a public account.", "Require a protected appropriations return."),
        "An Appropriation with an Account",
        "The final renewal reaches the chamber with the staff return already attached. The clerk has left space for an explicit receiving condition and a date on which the commitment must be reviewed. A minister complains that the form makes an ordinary undertaking look uncertain. The committee answers that an undertaking depending on finite staff is uncertain until those conditions are understood.\n\nThe settlement retains the chosen civilian or defence priority and its cost. It does not replace the chamber's judgment with an automatic renewal. The thin folder has become useful because its unanswered question now has an office responsible for returning to it."),
    "SAP_air_service": ("The Instructor's Second Copy",
        "An instructor arrives with two copies of the same return. One contains the account requested by command. The other includes an uncertain maintenance observation she was advised to discuss informally. She does not claim the observation proves a failure. She wants the service to preserve it long enough for somebody qualified to examine it.\n\nThe selected review culture determines how far the inquiry can open. A civilian member argues that confidence requires an account people outside the service can question. The technical inspector warns that early estimates can acquire an authority their authors never intended. The instructor asks that neither concern become a reason to throw away the second copy.\n\nThe government can publish a bounded professional return or protect the working debrief. The reply decides how these first lessons enter the record, and which office must answer if an uncertain observation is ignored.",
        ("Publish a bounded professional return.", "Protect the working debrief and its uncertainties."),
        "A Lesson Reaches the Maintenance Rota",
        "The instructor recognizes a line from her second copy in the final maintenance rota. It has acquired a responsible inspector and a receiving unit, while the uncertainty that prompted it remains stated rather than concealed. The service has not proved itself incapable of error. It has shown that a tentative warning can lead to ordinary work without first becoming a public accusation or an internal embarrassment.\n\nThe settlement retains the chosen open or restricted review culture with its continuing cost. Its conclusion is a professional route from instruction to maintenance, supported by a completed service period and a record that the next team can question."),
    "GEB_sovereignty": ("The Refusal Returned for Revision",
        "The receiving officer drafts a refusal and sends it for review. It is legally sufficient and practically useless: it tells the requesting office what the country will not do, but identifies no appeal route, alternative timetable or domestic service that can continue. A district clerk returns it with those omissions underlined.\n\nThe selected frontier policy establishes whether civilian control or protected defence transit receives priority. One official wants the first refusal published as a demonstration that national authority can explain its limits. Another prefers a protected file in which dispatchers can negotiate details without appearing to concede the principle. The receiving officer asks which office will answer the second letter if the first is misunderstood.\n\nThe government must choose how this initial inspection return is kept. No reply grants access through another country or alters an existing frontier.",
        ("Publish the refusal and its appeal route.", "Keep a protected receiving and dispatch file."),
        "Authority in the Receiving Book",
        "The final review includes a refusal that required no emergency meeting. The receiving officer could identify the responsible authority, the conditions that could be reconsidered and the domestic work that would continue in the meantime. The district clerk describes it as an unusually useful answer, even though the immediate request was not accepted.\n\nThe settlement preserves the chosen civil or defence priority and the resources it withholds from other tasks. Sovereignty becomes an ordinary administrative responsibility rather than a claim repeated whenever an office is uncertain. The second letter now has a place in the duty book and an official answerable for its return."),
    "GEB_recovery": ("The Workshop at the End of the List",
        "The recovery dispatcher reads the priority list from the top. A district representative asks him to start again from the bottom, where a small workshop has waited through several urgent reallocations. Its return is complete, but nobody has examined the effect of postponing it again. The foreman has sent a receiving schedule rather than another appeal.\n\nUnder the selected policy, district boards or the central dispatch control the next allocation. An inspector wants the uncompleted queue published so that repeated delays remain visible. A technical officer asks for a protected return that lets crews revise uncertain estimates without promising dates they cannot keep. The foreman's schedule supports both arguments: it states what the workshop can receive and leaves the dispatch date blank.\n\nThe first maintenance account can be public or ministerial. Either must preserve the waiting work as part of the nation's obligations.",
        ("Publish the uncompleted maintenance queue.", "Protect a technical return with revisable estimates."),
        "The Workshop Receives an Answer",
        "The workshop's receiving schedule returns with an answer. It is not the earliest date the foreman requested, but it names the office responsible for notifying him if the crew is reassigned. The final review records both the completed maintenance period and the work that remains. Nobody is permitted to count the same crew twice in order to make the total look better.\n\nThe settlement retains the chosen district or central priorities and their continuing cost. Recovery has acquired an ordinary obligation to answer a receiving office, release skilled staff and keep the uncompleted queue visible. The foreman files the answer beside the original blank schedule."),
}

FINALS = {
    "NPT": ("The Islands' Next Duty Book", "The final meeting begins with three records: the national mandate, the harbour allocation and the watch service. Their priorities are not identical, and the government has not asked the clerks to make them look identical. What they share is a receiving office, a completed period of work and a means of reviewing the next promise.\n\nThe harbour superintendent recognizes a cancellation that released a crew on time. The watch officer points to a question that remains unanswered. The committee chair says both belong in the assessment. A service becomes less credible when its record contains only success.\n\nThe government can protect a period of public maintenance review or reserve staff for a narrower readiness return. Each choice consumes attention or civilian resources. The enduring charters, their different priorities and their costs remain the national settlement; this assessment determines how the next service period will be supported."),
    "REC": ("A Republic of Receiving Offices", "The messenger who returned the first disputed register is invited to the final review. The administration has not resolved every claim. It can now show which office received each case, which maintenance period finished and which external questions remain unanswered. The messenger asks whether those responsibilities will survive the next reorganization.\n\nA council secretary points to the standing record; a works official points to the continuing allocation. Neither offers the file as a substitute for crews, materials or a sustainable budget. Their achievement is that a later government can change a priority without first losing the people waiting beneath it.\n\nThe republic can support a period of public maintenance review or a more concentrated technical return. The existing political and territorial settlement remains intact. The chosen national policies are now ordinary obligations, with continuing costs and records that residents can challenge."),
    "SAP": ("The Folder Is Returned to the Chamber", "The appropriations clerk returns the final folder without adding a ceremonial cover. It contains the national mandate, the professional service record and the receiving returns from completed work. A member asks whether the paperwork proves that the government chose correctly. The clerk answers that it proves who must explain the next choice.\n\nThe instructor finds her uncertain observation in the maintenance record and leaves it there. The committee has accepted that a credible assessment must preserve a question as well as a conclusion. The standing policies retain different balances between openness, discretion, civilian claims and readiness. Their costs have not disappeared because the review is complete.\n\nThe government can support a period of public maintenance scrutiny or reserve staff for a more concentrated professional readiness return. Either option is a limited commitment. Neither turns the closing meeting into permission to promise resources that the service does not possess."),
    "GEB": ("The Second Letter Has an Address", "At the final review the cabinet secretary places the original unanswered request beside the workshop's receiving schedule. Both now have an address for the next letter. One may still receive a refusal; the other may still wait for a crew. The administration's improvement is that neither uncertainty requires the state to invent a new authority before answering.\n\nThe receiving officer argues that the record should remain open to challenge. The dispatch representative asks that technical estimates remain revisable. The final charters preserve the government's chosen balance between those concerns and the continuing cost of maintaining it. Independence has become work performed by ordinary offices, not a promise that the country will accept every request.\n\nA period of public maintenance review can support the next return, or staff can be reserved for a narrower readiness account. The frontier, inherited obligations and the finite resources behind them remain the limits of that choice."),
}


def build_narrative(effects, triggers, loc, idea):
    from reconstruction.build import block, guard
    events = []; custom = []; dispatch = []; interrupted = []
    def event(number, tag, title, prose, condition, options, picture):
        ident = "ac_rc." + str(number); token = "ac_rc_message_" + str(number)
        sent, pending, window, answered = (token + suffix for suffix in ("_sent", "_pending", "_window", "_answered"))
        triggers[token + "_answerable"] = f"ac_rc_active = yes tag = {tag} original_tag = {tag} has_country_flag = {pending} has_country_flag = {window} NOT = {{ has_country_flag = {answered} }}"
        dispatch.append(guard(f"tag = {tag} ac_rc_active = yes {condition} NOT = {{ has_country_flag = {sent} }}",
            f"set_country_flag = {sent}\nset_country_flag = {pending}\nset_country_flag = {{ flag = {window} days = 60 }}\ncountry_event = {{ id = {ident} days = 1 }}"))
        interrupted.extend(["clr_country_flag = " + pending, "clr_country_flag = " + window])
        loc(ident + ".t", title); loc(ident + ".d", prose)
        body = f"id = {ident}\ntitle = {ident}.t\ndesc = {ident}.d\npicture = {picture}\nis_triggered_only = yes\nfire_only_once = yes\ntrigger = {{ {token}_answerable = yes }}\n"
        for n, (label, action) in enumerate(options):
            option = ident + "." + chr(97 + n); loc(option, label)
            apply = token + "_reply_" + str(n)
            effects[apply] = guard(token + "_answerable = yes", f"set_country_flag = {answered}\nclr_country_flag = {pending}\nclr_country_flag = {window}\n" + action)
            body += block("option", "name = " + option + "\nai_chance = { factor = " + ("55" if n == 0 else "45") + " }\n" + apply + " = yes")
        events.append(block("country_event", body))

    for offset, (tag, name) in enumerate(COUNTRY_NAMES.items()):
        title, prose, labels = OPENINGS[tag]
        choices = []
        for n, label in enumerate(labels):
            ident = tag + "_rc_opening_" + ("public" if n == 0 else "ministerial")
            values = {"stability_factor": 0.01, "political_power_gain": -0.03} if n == 0 else {"production_factory_efficiency_gain_factor": 0.015, "consumer_goods_factor": 0.005}
            idea(ident + "_spirit", name + (": An Open Hearing" if n == 0 else ": A Ministerial Docket"),
                 "Staff support the chosen opening review for ninety days. Its continuing political attention or civilian reservation remains a real cost.", values)
            choices.append((label, "set_country_flag = " + ident + "\nadd_timed_idea = { idea = " + ident + "_spirit days = 90 }"))
        ready = "OR = { " + " ".join("has_country_flag = " + focus_id(b,0) + "_done" for b in BRANCHES if b.tag == tag) + " }"
        event(1+offset, tag, title, prose, ready, choices, "GFX_ac_rc_event_" + tag.lower() + "_national")
    for offset, branch in enumerate(BRANCHES):
        bid = branch_id(branch); key = branch.tag + "_" + branch.key
        title, prose, labels, final_title, final_prose = SCENES[key]
        policy_name = bid + "_policy_callback"
        loc(policy_name + "_civil", "The standing policy is " + branch.policies[0][0].lower() + ". Its continuing cost remains part of the allocation.")
        loc(policy_name + "_defence", "The standing policy is " + branch.policies[1][0].lower() + ". Its continuing cost remains part of the allocation.")
        policy = "".join(block("text", "trigger = { has_country_flag = " + focus_id(branch,p+1) + "_done }\nlocalization_key = " + policy_name + ("_civil" if p == 0 else "_defence")) for p in range(2))
        custom.append(block("defined_text", "name = " + policy_name + "\n" + policy))
        choices = []
        for n, label in enumerate(labels):
            ident = bid + "_reply_" + ("public" if n == 0 else "ministerial")
            values = {k: round(v * .5,3) for k,v in branch.services[n][3].items()}
            if n == 0:
                values["consumer_goods_factor" if "political_power_gain" in values else "political_power_gain"] = .005 if "political_power_gain" in values else -.03
            else:
                # A smaller benefit is not a countervailing cost. Reserve a
                # different modifier so a stability-support reply still pays.
                for cost_key, cost in (("stability_factor", -.005), ("consumer_goods_factor", .005), ("political_power_gain", -.03)):
                    if cost_key not in values:
                        values[cost_key] = cost
                        break
                else:
                    raise ValueError("Reply requires a distinct opportunity cost: " + ident)
            idea(ident + "_spirit", branch.titles[0] + (": Public Return" if n == 0 else ": Protected Working Return"),
                 "This ninety-day response supports the chosen reporting procedure. " + branch.services[n][1] + " Public attention or reserved staff time remains committed to that work.", values)
            choices.append((label, "set_country_flag = " + ident + "\nadd_timed_idea = { idea = " + ident + "_spirit days = 90 }"))
        condition = "OR = { has_country_flag = " + focus_id(branch,1) + "_done has_country_flag = " + focus_id(branch,2) + "_done }"
        event(10+offset, branch.tag, title, prose + "\n\n[" + policy_name + "]", condition, choices, "GFX_ac_rc_event_" + bid)
        callback = bid + "_report_callback"
        loc(callback + "_public", "The earlier reply opened the return to public questions. The final assessment keeps the unresolved entries visible rather than removing them to produce a cleaner account.")
        loc(callback + "_ministerial", "The earlier reply protected a working ministerial return. The final assessment preserves its uncertainties and identifies the office answerable for any later correction.")
        loc(callback + "_unanswered", "No first reply was ratified. The final assessment states its reporting duty explicitly instead of inventing a choice that the government never made.")
        response = "".join(block("text", "trigger = { has_country_flag = " + bid + "_reply_" + kind + " }\nlocalization_key = " + callback + "_" + kind) for kind in ("public", "ministerial"))
        custom.append(block("defined_text", "name = " + callback + "\n" + response + block("text", "localization_key = " + callback + "_unanswered")))
        event(30+offset, branch.tag, final_title, final_prose + "\n\n[" + callback + "]\n\n[" + policy_name + "]",
              "has_country_flag = " + focus_id(branch,5) + "_done", [("File the completed service record.", "set_country_flag = " + bid + "_assessment_recorded")], "GFX_ac_rc_event_" + bid)
    for offset, (tag,name) in enumerate(COUNTRY_NAMES.items()):
        title, prose = FINALS[tag]; choices = []
        for n, label in enumerate(("Support a public maintenance review.", "Reserve staff for a focused readiness return.")):
            ident = tag + "_rc_final_" + ("public" if n == 0 else "readiness")
            benefit = {"NPT":{"air_mission_efficiency":.015},"REC":{"production_factory_efficiency_gain_factor":.02},
                       "SAP":{"experience_gain_air_factor":.025},"GEB":{"supply_consumption_factor":-.02}}[tag]
            values = {"repair_speed_factor":.025,"political_power_gain":-.05} if n == 0 else {**benefit,"consumer_goods_factor":.005}
            idea(ident + "_spirit", name + (": Public Maintenance Assessment" if n == 0 else ": Reserved Readiness Return"),
                 "The final national assessment supports the chosen return for ninety days. Its political or civilian opportunity cost remains, while the enduring policies retain their own continuing tradeoffs.",values)
            choices.append((label,"set_country_flag = " + ident + "\nadd_timed_idea = { idea = " + ident + "_spirit days = 90 }"))
        ready = " ".join("has_country_flag = " + focus_id(b,5) + "_done" for b in BRANCHES if b.tag == tag)
        callback = tag + "_rc_opening_callback"
        for kind, value in (("public","The opening hearing invited public questions. Its final account returns to that promise without pretending that every disagreement has been resolved."),
                            ("ministerial","The opening review began with a protected ministerial docket. Its final account identifies what that process delivered and who must answer for the work still waiting."),
                            ("unanswered","The opening invitation was never ratified. This assessment records that absence and proceeds from the services actually completed.")):
            loc(callback + "_" + kind,value)
        entries="".join(block("text","trigger = { has_country_flag = " + tag + "_rc_opening_" + kind + " }\nlocalization_key = " + callback + "_" + kind) for kind in ("public","ministerial"))
        custom.append(block("defined_text","name = " + callback + "\n" + entries + block("text","localization_key = " + callback + "_unanswered")))
        event(50+offset,tag,title,prose + "\n\n[" + callback + "]",ready,choices,"GFX_ac_rc_event_" + tag.lower() + "_national")
    effects["ac_rc_narrative_tick"] = "\n".join(dispatch)
    effects["ac_rc_interrupt_narrative"] = guard("ac_rc_participant = yes", "\n".join(interrupted))
    return "\n".join(events), "\n".join(custom)
